# AWPTCM-T44277 (214) Authentication&Security_... - Supplicant MAC
# Working log, run 1 -- tb277 / swi_d (AT-x960-56YSQ) -- group B, authmodes-2026-10-23T1304
# Tester: bench-runner subagent, 2026-10-23 15:41-15:54 NZDT (02:41-02:54 UTC)

## What the case asks, and what the feature turned out to be

No authored steps in `ck.db`. TPS row **213 "Supplicant MAC" reads `Yes`**. The dispatch set the
objective as: *pre-authorise a specific MAC and show that MAC passes traffic without
authenticating, while a different MAC on the same port does not, in the same run.*

🔴 **That framing is only half right, and finding out which half is the first result of this
case.** `auth supplicant-mac <MAC>` on its own is **neither a pre-authorisation nor a
restriction**. It creates a **per-supplicant-MAC override of the port's authentication
parameters**, and it is the `port-control` sub-option that produces the behaviour the dispatch
described. Both halves of the dispatch's pair were delivered -- a MAC that passes traffic
having sent no EAPOL frame at all, beside a MAC on the same port that does not -- but via
`auth supplicant-mac <MAC> port-control force-authorized`, and the bare form was measured
separately and shown to do neither thing.

Everything below is graded on frames: ARP requests sent and ARP replies received on the wire
(`tools/arpleg.py`), and EAPOL frame tables (`tools/eapol.py`). Nothing is graded on `show`;
`show` is used only as a second witness.

