Two MACs are used throughout, neither of them the host's own:

| name | MAC | sender IP | role |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **MAC A** | `02:0d:b9:00:42:77` (`020d.b900.4277`) | 10.38.22.70 | the MAC named in `auth supplicant-mac` |
| **MAC B** | `02:0d:b9:00:42:78` (`020d.b900.4278`) | 10.38.22.71 | the CONTROL -- never named in any command |

**The dataplane probe is an ARP request for the DUT's own vlan48 address** (`who-has
10.38.22.66 tell <sender>`, link-layer broadcast, 4 of them per leg) and **the measurement is
how many ARP replies come back**. It needs no next-hop MAC and no routing, and an unauthorized
port drops it exactly as it drops any other non-EAPOL frame.

🔴 **The zeros below are measurements, not a dead instrument.** The same `arpleg.py` invocation,
unchanged but for the source MAC, returned **3 replies** in STEP 2 -- so the instrument is
proven able to see a reply on this wire (memory `tcpdump-nfs-root-squash-capture`: a zero counts
only once the same instrument has given a non-zero).

Every authentication leg was preceded by `dot1x initialize interface port1.0.48` (group A's
method).

