## STEP 1 -- the bare command: `auth supplicant-mac <MAC A>` is NOT a pre-authorisation, and NOT a restriction

```
configure terminal / interface port1.0.48 / auth supplicant-mac 020d.b900.4277 / end

show running-config interface port1.0.48
 ...
 dot1x port-control auto
 dot1x control-direction both
 auth supplicant-mac 020d.b900.4277

show auth interface port1.0.48
  ...
  supplicantMac:
    MAC Address: 020d.b900.4277
      portControl: Auto
      reAuthenticate: disabled
      reAuthPeriod: 3600
      PAE: quietPeriod: 60 - maxReauthReq: 2
      BE: suppTimeout: 30 - serverTimeout: 30 - maxReq: 2

show auth supplicant interface port1.0.48 brief   ->   No supplicants
```

Two things were then measured, and both refute the obvious readings of the case title:

| leg | what it tests | result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| S1 MAC A, 4 x ARP, **no EAPOL at all** | *is the named MAC pre-authorised?* | **0 replies -- NO** |
| S1 MAC B, 4 x ARP, no EAPOL | (control) | **0 replies** |
| S2 **MAC B**, full EAP-MD5 as `ckmulti2`, alone on the port | *is the port restricted to the named MAC?* | **6 EAPOL frames, EAP-Success at 1.024 s -- NO** |

```
S2  pcap -> /tmp/authm1304/T44277-S2.pcap (6 EAPOL frames)
     0.003 02:0d:b9:00:42:78 -> EAPOL-Start
     0.008 02:0d:b9:00:42:78 <- EAP-Request/Identity id=1 len=0
     0.012 02:0d:b9:00:42:78 -> EAP-Response/Identity "ckmulti2"
     0.016 02:0d:b9:00:42:78 <- EAP-Request/MD5-Challenge id=2 len=17
     0.019 02:0d:b9:00:42:78 -> EAP-Response/MD5-Challenge digest=994b15d3592d8454715a66f0967dd8f2
     1.024 02:0d:b9:00:42:78 <- EAP-Success id=2
RESULT T44277-S2-OTHER-mac-valid-credentials: 1/1 matched -- MATCH
```

**STEP 1 verdict: PASS, and it is a correction worth recording.** Naming a MAC with
`auth supplicant-mac` **creates a parameter entry and changes no behaviour by itself**: the named
MAC still cannot pass traffic without authenticating, and a MAC that was never named still
authenticates normally and is admitted. `show auth supplicant brief` lists **No supplicants**
while the entry exists. Anyone reading the case title as "pre-authorise this MAC" or as
"only this MAC may authenticate" would write a wrong test and, on the bare command, get a wrong
answer in both directions.

