=========== ==== ==== ============== ================= =============== ========
port1.0.48  48   NONE 020d.b900.4277 ForceAuthorized   --              --
```

and in the DUT's own log: `802.1X[1094]: Force Authorized for 02-0d-b9-00-42-77 on port1.0.48`.

**STEP 2 verdict: PASS.** The named MAC passes traffic **without authenticating** -- it is
listed as a supplicant with Mode **`NONE`**, status **`ForceAuthorized`** and **no username**,
which is the DUT saying in its own words that no authentication took place. The unnamed MAC,
probed with the identical instrument in the same configuration seconds later, gets **nothing**.
The `otherAuthenticationSupplicantNum` counter moved to 1 while `dot1xAuthenticationSupplicantNum`
stayed 0.

**3 of 4, not 4 of 4, and why that is not a defect:** the first request of the leg was sent
while the switch was still creating the forced supplicant entry for a MAC it had not yet seen
(the entry appears on first frame -- `No supplicants` was read moments before). Requests 2, 3
and 4 were all answered. The discriminator is 3 versus 0, not 4 versus 3, and the control leg's
instrument was identical.

## STEP 3 -- `port-control force-unauthorized`: the named MAC is refused even with VALID credentials, while the control MAC authenticates

The reverse override, and the strongest proof that the entry is MAC-scoped rather than
port-scoped: the **same port**, the **same instant**, **valid credentials on both**.

```
configure terminal / interface port1.0.48
 auth supplicant-mac 020d.b900.4277 port-control force-unauthorized
end
show running-config interface port1.0.48 | include supplicant-mac
 auth supplicant-mac 020d.b900.4277 port-control force-unauthorized
dot1x initialize interface port1.0.48     ->   No supplicants
```

| leg | supplicant | credentials | EAPOL frames | outcome |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| S4c | **MAC A** (forced unauthorized) | `ckmulti1` / correct | **1** (its own EAPOL-Start) | **total silence for 40 s** |
| S4d | **MAC A**, 4 x ARP | n/a | n/a | **0 ARP replies** |
| S4b | **MAC B** (control) | `ckmulti2` / correct | **6** | **EAP-Success at 1.025 s** |

```
S4c  pcap (1 EAPOL frame)
     0.003 02:0d:b9:00:42:77 -> EAPOL-Start
  02:0d:b9:00:42:77  expected failure, got timeout -- MISMATCH

S4b  pcap (6 EAPOL frames)
     0.003 02:0d:b9:00:42:78 -> EAPOL-Start
     0.009 02:0d:b9:00:42:78 <- EAP-Request/Identity id=1 len=0
     0.013 02:0d:b9:00:42:78 -> EAP-Response/Identity "ckmulti2"
     0.017 02:0d:b9:00:42:78 <- EAP-Request/MD5-Challenge id=2 len=17
     0.021 02:0d:b9:00:42:78 -> EAP-Response/MD5-Challenge digest=ecfea37548e6ef95d50fe596c6f4cef5
     1.025 02:0d:b9:00:42:78 <- EAP-Success id=2
  RESULT T44277-S4b-CONTROL-other-mac-alone: 1/1 matched -- MATCH
```

DUT side: `port1.0.48 48 NONE 020d.b900.4277 ForceUnauthorized -- --`, and in the log
`802.1X[1094]: Force Unauthorized for 02-0d-b9-00-42-77 on port1.0.48`.

**STEP 3 verdict: PASS.** `force-unauthorized` on one MAC refuses that MAC and **only** that MAC.

⚠️ **The tool's `MISMATCH` on S4c is the honest answer and is not a failure of the case.** The
leg was declared `--expect failure`, i.e. an EAP-Failure frame. **The DUT does not send one** --
it does not even send an `EAP-Request/Identity`. The refusal is **silence**: the EAPOL-Start is
simply never answered. `eapol.py` grades neither-Success-nor-Failure as `timeout`, which is the
truthful reading of a wire that carried one frame in 40 seconds. Recorded as measured rather
than smoothed over.

⚠️ **One leg had to be re-run, and the reason is a known property of this bench, not a product
fault.** S4 first put **both** MACs in one `eapol.py` invocation (the pair in a single packet
window, which is the preferred shape). Both timed out -- including MAC B, which authenticates
reliably when run alone. That is `auth host-mode single-host` doing what group A measured in
T33360: with two EAPOL-Starts 3 ms apart on a single-host port, the port is taken and the other
supplicant gets no reply at all; here the forced-unauthorized entry took the slot and MAC B got
the silence. **The legs were therefore re-run one at a time with `dot1x initialize` between**,
and that is what is graded. The combined run is kept as `/tmp/authm1304/T44277-S4.pcap`. **The
pair is still a pair** -- same configuration, same minute, same instrument, 100 seconds apart --
it just is not a single packet window, and host-mode is why.

## 🔥 PRODUCT FINDING -- the per-MAC parameter entry is SINGLE-VALUED: a second `auth supplicant-mac <MAC> <param>` SILENTLY DISCARDS the first

Found while setting up a per-MAC re-authentication leg, and then isolated deliberately. **Four
reads, each immediately after its own command, nothing else changed:**

```
(1) no auth supplicant-mac 020d.b900.4277
    auth supplicant-mac 020d.b900.4277 reauthentication
      running-config:  auth supplicant-mac 020d.b900.4277 reauthentication
      show auth:       MAC Address: 020d.b900.4277
                         portControl: Auto
                         reAuthenticate: ENABLED          <-- the override works
                         reAuthPeriod: 3600

(2) auth supplicant-mac 020d.b900.4277 reauth-period 15
      running-config:  auth supplicant-mac 020d.b900.4277 reauth-period 15      <-- ONE line only
      show auth:       MAC Address: 020d.b900.4277
                         portControl: Auto
                         reAuthenticate: DISABLED         <-- (1) IS GONE
                         reAuthPeriod: 15

(3) auth supplicant-mac 020d.b900.4277 reauthentication
      running-config:  auth supplicant-mac 020d.b900.4277 reauthentication
      show auth:         reAuthenticate: enabled
                         reAuthPeriod: 3600               <-- (2) IS GONE, symmetrically
```

**No warning, no `% ` line, no log entry.** The entry holds exactly one parameter: each new
`auth supplicant-mac <MAC> <param>` **replaces** the whole entry rather than adding to it, in
both directions. An earlier three-command attempt (`port-control auto`, then `reauthentication`,
then `reauth-period 15`) left **only** `reauth-period 15` in the running-config, which is how
this was noticed.

**The functional consequence is real, not cosmetic:** the per-MAC re-authentication override
**cannot be used at any period other than the 3600 s default**, because configuring the period
turns the re-authentication off again. The same applies to every other pair of parameters.

This is the **same shape** as the campaign's existing finding that
`auth host-mode multi-supplicant` silently rewrites `dot1x control-direction both` to `in`
(queue `## Issues`), and as the known AW+ behaviour where a `spanning-tree mode` change silently
drops `no spanning-tree <mode> enable`: **a setting the operator entered is discarded without
being told.** Here it is worse in one respect -- the discarded setting is not even visible as a
second running-config line to notice.

**NOT established** (it needs a developer or the command reference, neither available here):
whether single-valuedness is intended -- i.e. whether the grammar is meant to be
`auth supplicant-mac <MAC> [one-parameter]` with repeated commands replacing, or whether
parameters were meant to accumulate. **Either way the operator is not told**, and the
`reauthentication` + `reauth-period` combination is unreachable. The behavioural confirmation at
the default period was **not run**: it would need a 3600-second window, which this unattended
campaign cannot spend. The configuration-level A/B above is reproducible in four commands.

