## Not run, and why

- **A behavioural leg for the per-MAC `quiet-period`, `supp-timeout`, `max-req` or
  `max-reauth-req` overrides.** Each is measurable the same way (a per-MAC value differing from
  the port-wide one, timed on the wire), and `quiet-period` is the cheapest (~30 s per leg: fail
  a supplicant, retry, see which one is still Held). Two per-MAC overrides were already proved
  behaviourally, so these were left for a next run rather than spent from an unattended window
  with three cases still queued.
- **The `mask` sub-option** (a MAC range rather than a single MAC) was found in the help and not
  exercised.
- **Persistence across a reboot**, for the same reason as T33374: proving it needs `write`, and
  this group's restore contract keeps `startup-config` at `flash:/default.cfg` so that a reload
  is itself a complete restore of group A's rig. Appearance in and disappearance from
  `show running-config` was proved instead.
- **A behavioural confirmation of the single-valued-entry finding** -- see that section.

VERDICT: PASS -- `auth supplicant-mac` works, and works as a per-MAC override of the port's authentication parameters rather than as the pre-authorisation or restriction the case title suggests: with `port-control force-authorized` the named MAC got 3 of 4 ARP replies from the DUT having sent zero EAPOL frames and is listed `Mode NONE / ForceAuthorized / no username`, while the unnamed control MAC got 0 of 4 on the same port with the same instrument; with `port-control force-unauthorized` the named MAC was refused in total silence even with valid credentials (1 EAPOL frame in 40 s, 0 ARP replies) while the control MAC authenticated to EAP-Success at 1.025 s; the bare command was measured to do neither thing; removal returned the named MAC to 0 of 4; and one defect-shaped finding is recorded -- the per-MAC entry is single-valued, so a second `auth supplicant-mac <MAC> <param>` silently discards the first and the `reauthentication` + `reauth-period` combination is unreachable.
